// src/v4/extdeps/formats/json.dag
//
// Scope: JSON data format model — direction-agnostic.
// Anchor: https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc8259 (RFC 8259) + https://ecma-international.org/publications-and-standards/standards/ecma-404/ (ECMA-404)
//
// Owns:
//   - JsonValue = Null | Bool | Number | String | Array | Object (closed sum)
//   - Object = Map<String, JsonValue>
//   - Array = List<JsonValue>
//   - Number model (RFC 8259 §6: arbitrary-precision OR IEEE-754; v4 picks IEEE-754 default + BigDecimal opt-in)
//   - parse(String) -> Result<JsonValue, Diagnostic>
//   - emit(JsonValue) -> String
//
// Consumes:
//   - std/node.dag, std/diagnostic.dag, std/collection.dag, std/algebra.dag (FreeMonoid)
//
// Status: scaffold — fill per TASKS.md T-4.6
// Brief: see BRIEF_TEMPLATE.md; this file's I/O contract is the immutable
// contract for the worker. Splitting this file requires explicit operator
// ratification (substrate extension = stop signal).

module v4.extdeps.formats.json


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Modeling notes (read before validating against the anchor)
//
// CANONICAL-FIRST SEAM (operator-ratified T-4 directive 2026-05-16:
// "one canonical file first, get its declarative model + the carrier-
// shaped seams ratified, THEN fan the rest out"). json.dag is the T-4.6
// reference: the other five formats (yaml/csv/toml/json_schema/openapi)
// mirror THIS file's value-model shape + parse/emit carrier seam — they
// do not each re-invent one (that re-invention IS the v3 cross-layer-
// drift failure mode v4 exists to kill). Surface this file for seam-
// review before fan-out.
//
// DECLARATIVE-NOW / OPERATIONS-DEFERRED-WITH-OWNER (the operator-
// sanctioned middle path, verified on std/collection.dag #3169). What
// is modeled here NOW: the `JsonValue` data model — declaration-class,
// substrate-faithful, no unlanded dependency. What is DEFERRED with a
// named owner + dissolution trigger (NEVER improvised, NEVER a fake
// stub, NEVER a bodiless `fn` — the frozen v2 seed rejects bodiless
// `fn`, DECISIONS PARSE-1): the `parse`/`emit` operation bodies and the
// numeric interpretation of a number lexeme. Both ride substrate that
// is scaffold today (std/text.dag for the Char-stream walk; std/float.
// dag + std/integer.dag for numeric interpretation). The deferral is a
// 🟡 TRACKED-SCAFFOLD block with the three bridge properties, exactly
// the collection.dag-deferred-Map pattern.
//
// CARRIER SEAM = Outcome<JsonValue> (NOT a per-file sum). The header's
// `parse(String) -> Result<JsonValue, Diagnostic>` prose denotes the
// operator-ratified D1 carrier `Outcome<T> = Produced { value: T } |
// Rejected { diagnostic: Diagnostic }` (std/diagnostic.dag, DECISIONS
// item **I**, 2026-05-16; `Result<…, Diagnostic>` prose names this same
// surface — there is no parallel `Result<ok, err>` in std/). When the
// parse op is authored (trigger below), it returns `Outcome<JsonValue>`.
// This is the cross-file seam the canonical-first directive ratifies on
// THIS file; the other five formats consume the SAME carrier.
//
// FAIL-CLOSED, NO FABRICATION (RFC 8259; brief §C binding STOP). Every
// non-conforming input is `Rejected { diagnostic }` — never a partial
// parse, never a defaulted/guessed JsonValue, never a fabricated number.
// Duplicate object names are structurally addressed: `JsonObject`'s
// carrier is `Map<String, JsonValue>`, which cannot hold a duplicate
// key — so a parsed `JsonObject` is dup-free BY CONSTRUCTION (illegal
// states unrepresentable, INVARIANTS P2). RFC 8259 §4 ("names within an
// object SHOULD be unique") + the no-engine discipline make duplicate-
// name INPUT a parse `Rejected`, not a silent last-wins resolution
// (silent resolution = the no-engine STOP) — enforced in the deferred
// parse body, structurally guaranteed by the carrier here.
//
// `Bool`/`String` are kernel-ambient (v2 seed; STRUCTURE.md "Kernel-
// ambient types"); `List<T>`/`Map<K,V>` are the kernel container types
// (node.dag uses `List` unimported). That relaxes only the import edge —
// this file remains JSON's sole modeling authority (the std/logic.dag
// kernel-ambient discipline). No std/ import is needed: the data model
// uses only kernel-ambient carriers + self-recursion; the deferred
// carrier seam is named in prose, not structurally referenced (an
// unused import would itself fail 0-diagnostics).


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// JsonValue — the RFC 8259 §3 JSON value: the closed, recursive set of
// the six JSON value kinds. Anchor: RFC 8259 §3 ("A JSON value MUST be
// an object, array, number, or string, or one of the following three
// literal names: false / null / true") + ECMA-404.
//
//   - JsonNull            — the `null` literal (RFC 8259 §3).
//   - JsonBool            — the `true` / `false` literals; the payload
//                           is the kernel `Bool` (one authority for the
//                           truth value — std/logic.dag — not a second
//                           True/False enum re-declared here).
//   - JsonNumber          — RFC 8259 §6. Carries the VERBATIM source
//                           number token (`lexeme`), NOT a numeric
//                           value. RFC 8259 §6 deliberately separates
//                           the number TOKEN (a lexical grammar
//                           production: `[minus] int [frac] [exp]`)
//                           from its numeric INTERPRETATION, which it
//                           leaves implementation-defined ("This
//                           specification allows implementations to set
//                           limits on the range and precision"). The
//                           faithful numeric carriers — IEEE-754
//                           `Float` (std/float.dag) and the integer
//                           tower (std/integer.dag) — are scaffold
//                           today, so collapsing the token to a numeric
//                           value NOW would (i) fabricate a precision
//                           decision the substrate has not landed
//                           (no-fabrication / no-engine) and (ii) lose
//                           information the round-trip (B-6) needs.
//                           Preserving the lexeme is the no-fabrication-
//                           correct, round-trip-exact choice; the
//                           numeric interpretation is a DEFERRED read
//                           owned by the numeric substrate (🟡 block
//                           below). The lexeme is a faithful external-
//                           format token, NOT an opaque-string heuristic
//                           channel (feedback_opaque_strings): nothing
//                           here branches on its spelling — its §6-
//                           conformance and its value are separately-
//                           owned deferred reads, exactly like a source
//                           span is preserved verbatim and interpreted
//                           by its own owner.
//   - JsonString          — RFC 8259 §7. The payload is the kernel
//                           `String`. (Escape decoding / `\uXXXX`
//                           surrogate-pair handling is a parse-body
//                           concern — the deferred Char-stream walk —
//                           not a data-model variant.)
//   - JsonArray           — RFC 8259 §5. An ordered sequence of
//                           JsonValue; the carrier is the kernel
//                           `List<JsonValue>` (order is the list order —
//                           the single authority, canonical/
//                           deterministic per CULTURE §8).
//   - JsonObject          — RFC 8259 §4. An unordered collection of
//                           name/value pairs; the carrier is the kernel
//                           `Map<String, JsonValue>` — which makes
//                           duplicate names structurally unrepresentable
//                           (see Modeling notes: dup-name INPUT is a
//                           parse `Rejected`, the parsed value is
//                           dup-free by construction).
//
// Recursion lives in the `elements` / `members` children (node.dag A1:
// the one recursive type is `Node`; this recursive `Disj` is a finite
// Node tree whose recursive occurrence is the name-reference `JsonValue`
// resolved through the namespace — never a self-referential type slot,
// exactly std/nat.dag's `Succ { prev: Nat }`). The recursion is REGULAR
// (the recursive occurrence is `JsonValue` itself, not a different
// instantiation) ⇒ unconditionally admissible per A1. Structural
// descent on a proper sub-JsonValue (a list element / map value) is the
// A2 IMPLICIT termination case — every fold/walk over a JsonValue
// strips one constructor layer, total by construction, no ranking
// measure needed.
//
// CLOSED. The six are RFC 8259 §3's exhaustive value grammar — there is
// no `Other` / open variant. A seventh JSON value kind would be an edit
// to this declaration routed to ratification (it would mean RFC 8259
// itself changed), not a silent escape.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / INVARIANTS P1 / modeling-
// discipline.md §4) — 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns
// attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: the value kind is consumed UNIFORMLY by
//      every JSON consumer (the deferred parse builds it, emit walks it,
//      json_schema validates over it, any pipeline ingest dispatches on
//      it). It is the shared classifier of the whole JSON value space,
//      not a per-consumer scattered fact.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: the six carry structurally DISTINCT
//      payloads (JsonNull nullary; a `Bool`; a lexeme `String`; a
//      `String`; a `List<JsonValue>`; a `Map<String, JsonValue>`).
//      Collapsing to a flat `{ kind, bool?, number?, string?, array?,
//      object? }` record makes illegal mixed states representable (a
//      JsonNull carrying array elements; a JsonBool with object
//      members) — the inverse of INVARIANTS P2 (illegal states
//      unrepresentable).
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: a JSON value is RFC 8259
//      §3's closed value grammar; it is heterogeneous external data,
//      not the carrier of a richer std/algebra.dag structure (no
//      Monoid/Ring/Lattice it inhabits). The Array/Object children
//      ground in their OWN carriers (List = FreeMonoid, Map) owned by
//      std/collection.dag — not a richer single source JsonValue
//      decomposes into; pointing the variants at an algebra is circular.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a JSON value is EXACTLY ONE of the six
//      kinds (RFC 8259 §3 — a value is one of object/array/number/
//      string/true/false/null); mutually-exclusive alternatives, never
//      simultaneous coordinates. No value is "both a number and an
//      array".
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the six variants are structurally
//      heterogeneous (nullary; `Bool`; lexeme `String`; `String`;
//      `List<JsonValue>`; `Map<String, JsonValue>`) — NOT one generic
//      `F<X>` / projection over a separately-declared enumerable set
//      (Practice 7). They are the irreducible constructors of the RFC
//      8259 §3 value grammar, not a mechanical copy of an enumerable
//      set.
// Terminal: the irreducible closed RFC 8259 §3 JSON value grammar; a
// recursive Disj whose Array/Object children recurse through kernel
// `List`/`Map` of `JsonValue` (A1 recursion-in-children, name-reference
// through the namespace — the std/nat.dag `Nat` shape).
type JsonValue
  = JsonNull
  | JsonBool { value: Bool }
  | JsonNumber { lexeme: String }
  | JsonString { value: String }
  | JsonArray { elements: List<JsonValue> }
  | JsonObject { members: Map<String, JsonValue> }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Deferred — TRACKED SCAFFOLD (🟡). The operations ride substrate that
// is scaffold today. Per the operator-ratified declarative-now /
// operations-deferred-with-owner discipline (verified on std/collection.
// dag #3169) these are NOT authored here — not as improvised bodies,
// not as bodiless `fn` signatures (the frozen v2 seed rejects bodiless
// `fn`, DECISIONS PARSE-1: "expected LBrace"). Each carries the three
// bridge properties (scaffold doc / bounds on use / dissolution
// trigger).
//
// (1) `json_parse` / `json_emit` — the parse and emit operation bodies.
//   RFC 8259 §2: a JSON text is a sequence of Unicode code points; a
//   JSON parser is fundamentally a Char-STREAM structural walk and emit
//   builds a String from structure. Both require std/text.dag's `Char`
//   + a `String` ↔ sequence-of-`Char` decomposition — std/text.dag is
//   T-3 SCAFFOLD (unmerged). RATIFIED parse strategy (the brief §C
//   "recursive vs iterative" decision, settled here as the canonical
//   seam): RECURSIVE-DESCENT — a single self-recursive structural walk
//   descending on the input Char-sequence spine (RFC 8259's grammar is
//   LL(1)), mirroring `JsonValue`'s own recursion; termination is the
//   A2 IMPLICIT case (descent on a proper sub-sequence of the input
//   list — the std/node.dag `node_well_formed` shape), no ranking
//   measure needed.
//     (a) scaffold doc — THIS note; `json_parse`/`json_emit` are
//         intentionally absent from json.dag today.
//     (b) bounds on use — no consumer reads `json_parse`/`json_emit`
//         from json.dag in this PR. The carrier-shaped seam IS ratified
//         here: when authored, `json_parse : String -> Outcome<JsonValue>`
//         and `json_emit : JsonValue -> String`, where `Outcome<T>` is
//         the operator-ratified D1 carrier (std/diagnostic.dag,
//         DECISIONS item **I**) — fail-closed (every non-conforming
//         input is `Rejected { diagnostic }`, never a partial/defaulted
//         value; brief §C binding STOP). The other five T-4.6 formats
//         mirror THIS seam (one carrier, not a per-file sum).
//     (c) dissolution trigger — when std/text.dag (T-3) lands its
//         `Char` + `String` ↔ `List<Char>` decomposition, a follow-up
//         PR adds `json_parse`/`json_emit` as single self-recursive
//         structural walks returning `Outcome<JsonValue>`; this note
//         dissolves. The T-4 directive's "operations deferred-with-
//         named-owner" + TASKS.md T-4.6 record the same dependency.
//
// (2) Numeric interpretation of `JsonNumber.lexeme` — RFC 8259 §6
//   separates the number TOKEN (carried verbatim above) from its
//   numeric VALUE, which the spec leaves implementation-defined. The
//   faithful numeric carriers are scaffold: IEEE-754 `Float`
//   (std/float.dag) and the integer tower (std/integer.dag), both T-3.
//   The header's "IEEE-754 default + BigDecimal opt-in" is the eventual
//   interpretation POLICY — it is ratified and authored once those
//   carriers land, not fabricated here.
//     (a) scaffold doc — THIS note; `JsonNumber` carries the lexeme,
//         not a numeric value; no interpretation is claimed.
//     (b) bounds on use — a consumer needing the numeric value waits on
//         the interpretation read. The lexeme is round-trip-exact
//         (B-6): emit reproduces the source token with zero precision
//         loss, which a premature IEEE-754 collapse could not.
//     (c) dissolution trigger — when std/float.dag + std/integer.dag
//         land, a follow-up adds `json_number_value : JsonNumber ->
//         Outcome<…>` per the header's IEEE-754-default / BigDecimal-
//         opt-in policy; this note dissolves.

